<article class="env-lemma" id="0C4R">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C4R">Lemma <span data-tag="0C4R">31.14.10</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme. Let $D, D' \subset X$ be effective Cartier divisors such that the scheme theoretic intersection $D \cap D'$ is an effective Cartier divisor on $D'$. Then $D + D'$ is the scheme theoretic union of $D$ and $D'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      See Morphisms, Definition <a href="/tag/0C4H" data-tag="0C4H">29.4.4</a> for the definition of scheme theoretic intersection and union. To prove the lemma working locally (using Lemma <a href="/tag/01WS" data-tag="01WS">31.14.2</a>) we obtain the following algebra problem: Given a ring $A$ and nonzerodivisors $f_1, f_2 \in A$ such that $f_1$ maps to a nonzerodivisor in $A/f_2A$, show that $f_1A \cap f_2A = f_1f_2A$. We omit the straightforward argument. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>